questions.question:
Statement log of PostgreSQL (log_statement = 'all') for one learner, laptop request A and phone request B:

10:02:11.004 [A] SELECT version, lessons FROM course_progress WHERE user_id='u_an' AND course_id='ts-101';  -> 6, {"ts-1":40}
10:02:11.006 [B] SELECT version, lessons FROM course_progress WHERE user_id='u_an' AND course_id='ts-101';  -> 6, {"ts-1":40}
10:02:11.019 [A] UPDATE course_progress SET version=7, lessons='{"ts-1":40,"ts-2":100}' WHERE user_id='u_an' AND course_id='ts-101';
10:02:11.021 [B] UPDATE course_progress SET version=7, lessons='{"ts-1":60}' WHERE user_id='u_an' AND course_id='ts-101';

Statement: "Wrapping each request's SELECT and UPDATE in BEGIN ... COMMIT at PostgreSQL's default isolation level (READ COMMITTED) is enough to stop B from erasing A's ts-2."
